e median  household income this makes  homeownership basically impossible for  the average person or family in nyc and  that's why most people don't even  consider buying a property in the city  usually most of them just rent for their  entire lives and given that real estate  ownership is responsible for creating 90  of all millionaires in the world this is  not the best of signs for the people of  nyc to make things worse and it's not  like renting is that much cheaper a  studio apartment itself will cost you 3  638 per month on average that translates  to over 43 000 per year which is higher  than the median annual income in the  city and that's just a studio if you  want a one bedroom you're looking at  four thousand seven hundred sixty one  dollars per month or fifty seven  thousand dollars per year if you want a  two bedroom you're looking at six  thousand one hundred and eighty eight  dollars per month or seventy four  thousand dollars per year and if you  want a three bedroom you're looking at  six thousand five hundred forty four  dollars per month or 78 000 per year  obviously none of these options are  affordable for the average resident in  nyc  i mean the rent for a two bedroom  apartment is literally a thousand  dollars more expensive than the mortgage  and tax that you would have to pay for  an 800 000 house if you could afford  this by yourself you might as well just  buy a house most people in nyc can't  afford it and that's why almost all of  the average folks have roommates in fact  40 of all adult renters in new york live  with roommates aside from having to  share your apartment and still pay  insane amounts in rent you also have to  deal with extremely old buildings the  average residential building in nyc is  literally 90 years old this not only  makes these buildings dingy and  depressing but they're usually infested  with mice rants and cockroaches and all  of that is just a housing situation  almost every facet of life is just as  expensive in nyc  groceries for example cost 486 dollars  per person per month on average the  average across the us is only 348 or 30  lower hearing all this i don't think  you'd be surprised to hear the 60 of new  yorkers live paycheck to paycheck  clearly the living situation for the  average new yorker isn't anything to  brag about and this has prompted many to  ditch the state  historically one of the top reasons that  people immigrated to nyc was because of  all the job opportunities that are  available in the city no other city in  the world offered the same networking  capabilities when it came to the  financial markets or hustling your way  to the top with the rise of technology  over the past few decades however the  appeal of nyc has been on a decline take  the stock market for example before 2000  the trading floor of the new york stock  exchange was crucial to operating the  stock market but with the rise of the  nasdaq and electronic trading and  execution trading forests have basically  become obsolete you no longer need to be  in new york to make it in the financial  world in fact most of the fintech  companies that we hear about nowadays  weren't even created in new york this  includes robin hood coinbase square  allied bank acorn stripe and so many  more  and if you don't have to be in new york  to build a financial career why put up  with all the cons that come along with  the region  also something else to note is that you  no longer have to be involved in the  financial markets at all to even become  wealthy 50 to 100 years ago the most  straightforward way to earn a high  salary was to go to new york join a  financial firm and work up the corporate  ladder but now there's an industry that  not only pays more but is also less  intense and less stressful and that's of  course tech  nowadays the dream career for many is to  get a job at fang or an up-and-coming  startup and this makes sense given how  much these companies pay i'm sure you've  all heard about software engineers  making multiple six figures but nowadays  top software engineers can even earn  seven figures now to google for instance  even if you're just an entry-level  software engineer you earn a total  compensation of 191 000  meanwhile if you work up to being a  principal engineer you can earn a total  compensation of 1.08 million dollars  we can see an extremely similar  compensation plan at facebook as well  even if you're just an entry-level  software engineer you earn a total  compensation of 184 000  meanwhile if you work update being a  principal engineer you can earn up to  1.61 million dollars now of course  working up to principal engineer is no  easy task but it's also rather  achievable if you stick with your  company for 20 to 30 years  also even if you don't make it to level  8 and you get stuck at level 6 or 7  you're still pulling in well over half a  million dollars per year and that's in  today's dollars by the time most of us  reach our 50s and 60s these positions  themselves will likely be paying well  over a million dollars so finance is no  longer the only way to make big money  without starting a business not to  mention while these tech jobs are by no  means easy you probably won't be working  80 to 100 hours per week either like  investment bankers and hedge fund people  you'll probably be working closer to 60  hours which makes tech jobs even more  appealing so the rise of tech has robbed  new york of much of its wall street  appeal  aside from a high cost of living and  deteriorating appeal a major drawback of  nyc is that it has never really been a  final destination a lot of people are  attracted to the city by its fast life  and career opportunities but more times  than not these people don't intend to  stay for the rest of their lives the  vast majority of individuals who are  immigrating to new york can usually be  categorized into two bins the queer  builders and the adventurers the career  builders usually go their straight out  of college or they might even go there  for college itself these guys usually  get a job at a financial firm or  consulting firm or they might even start  their own company then they grind out  most of their 20s in nyc and end up  saving multiple six figures if not even  seven figures and finally they move back  to their home city or an up and coming  suburb where they get a job as a  financial analyst or an accountant and  start a family meanwhile the adventurers  are broke throughout their entire  twenties but they get the satisfaction  that they lived their life to the  fullest while they were still young and  free however these guys also usually end  up moving to suburbs as they grow older  in other words nyc is rarely a final  destination and this makes sense given  the pros and cons of the city while the  city offers great career opportunities  and a fast life it's not exactly the  best place to raise a family cities in  general usually don't have great public  schools because they don't get very good  funding in fact schools in urban  districts get 2 100 less per student  than schools in suburban districts  considering this it's not surprising  that parents are usually reluctant to  send kids to public schools in major  cities even if you find a good school  the environment itself is usually not  the best for kids crime rates are high  subways aren't the safest and there's no  neighborhood swimming pool or park and  this is especially problem if you're  living in poorer areas of the city  aside from it being hard to raise a  family new york isn't the best place to  settle down as an individual either  first of all the weather is pretty bad  winters are often brutal given that new  york receives the seventh most amount of  snowfall in the entire country this is  the exact opposite of california  californians often say that weather is  one of the top reasons that they want to  stay in california but maybe they're  overstating this advantage given that  people have been moving to new york for  centuries despite its bad weather  anyway aside from poor weather you also  have taxes that will make you poor state  income taxes range from 4 up to 10.9  combine this with federal income taxes  and high cost of living and you'll be  broke before you know it while this may  be an acceptable sacrifice throughout  your 20s this gets old real fast as you  grow older and sooner or later most  people just want something more slow  paced and relaxed which usually leads  them to suburbs  in the end nyc has historically been one  of the most desirable cities in the  world  people from all around the world have  been immigrating to the city since the  beginning of america and there's still  plenty of people moving to city to build  their careers and lives but over the  past several years the number of people  leaving the city and state has outpaced  a number of people moving to the state  you could list a slew of reasons for  this from bad weather to poor schools  but at the end of the day it just comes  down to one reason less and less people  think that the opportunities offered by  new york are worth the high cost  nowadays you can progress within the  financial world without having to live  in new york not to mention it you don't  even have to join the financial world to  make large salaries anymore technology  and the internet have created more  hyping opportunities than ever before  the fact that the billionaire's list and  the fortune 500 are dominated by tech is  simply perfect evidence of this this  isn't to say that new york is gonna fall  towards irrelevancy or something like  that new york is still filled with  plenty of career opportunities some of  which are still unique to new york like  consulting also there's a lot of young  souls who still want to have an  adventure in new york before they settle  down in a regular old suburb so while  new york is the fastest drinking state  right now this is just natural shifts  due to changing opportunities sooner or  later new york will settle down at a new  population level which will likely help  with over congestion and affordability  and hopefully this will open up the  state to a whole new generation of  people but that's just what i think  would you live in new york comment that  down below  also drop a like if you're all about the  chosen bourbon life and of course  consider checking out our discord  community to just for your video ideas  and consider subscribing and see more  questions logically answered but until  then i'm hari and i'll see you guys on  the next one  you

